From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Bongaarts <cab@bongalow.net>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 02:28:15 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] 10218: Decompiler: label overridden switches by their two
 table indices for table-of-tables dispatch

Decompiler: label overridden table-of-tables switches by
 their two indices

A computed jump that reads its target through a table of tables
(ptr = T[outer]; target = ptr[inner]) cannot be recovered by switch
analysis: no single varnode produces every destination. With a manual
jump-table override the decompiler fell back to
JumpBasicOverride::setupTrivial, so the switch was printed on the loaded
pointer and every case label was a target address.

Add a fallback inside JumpBasicOverride::recoverModel, after trialNorm
fails and before setupTrivial. It finds the two free inputs of the
address calculation (an input, a MULTIEQUAL, or a LOAD of a variable; a
LOAD whose pointer depends on another LOAD, or adds a constant base in
read-only memory, is a table read and is evaluated instead). The inner
input must reach the target through exactly one LOAD and the outer
through exactly two; a longer chain keeps the trivial model. Both are
enumerated over their non-zero masks, evaluating the BRANCHIND input
with tables read from read-only memory in the load image. Every override
destination must be produced, or the trivial model is used exactly as
before. Labels are (outer << k) | inner, where k is the inner index's
mask width; foldInNormalization builds that composite as the switch
variable. A new JumpModel::suppressDefault() keeps JumpTable::switchOver
from choosing a default case, because the labels list every table entry.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01NvtBaQBU5K9LiCeqXgAhG3
